      "modelAnswer": "Before a cell divides, its DNA is copied so that each daughter cell receives an identical copy. The enzyme DNA helicase unwinds the double helix and breaks the hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs, so the two polynucleotide strands separate. Each strand now acts as a template. Free DNA nucleotides in the nucleus are attracted to their complementary exposed bases on each template, adenine pairing with thymine and cytosine with guanine, and are held there by hydrogen bonds; these hydrogen bonds form without an enzyme. DNA polymerase then joins adjacent nucleotides of each new strand by catalysing condensation reactions between the phosphate of one nucleotide and the deoxyribose of the next, forming phosphodiester bonds and a new sugar-phosphate backbone. Because DNA polymerase can only add nucleotides in one direction and the two strands are antiparallel, one new strand is made continuously while the other is made in short sections, which DNA ligase joins together by forming further phosphodiester bonds. Each new DNA molecule then rewinds into a double helix. Each contains one strand from the original molecule and one newly made strand, which is why the process is called semi-conservative. Complementary base pairing means the base sequence of each new molecule is the same as the original."
