      "command": "Explain",
      "modelAnswer": "All three are polysaccharides made by condensation reactions between glucose molecules, but differences in the type of glucose and the glycosidic bonds give them very different properties. Starch is the energy store of plants and is made of α-glucose. It has two components. Amylose is an unbranched chain of α-glucose joined by α-1,4 glycosidic bonds; the angle of these bonds makes the chain coil into a helix held by hydrogen bonds, which makes it compact, so a large amount of glucose can be stored in a small space in plastids. Amylopectin also has α-1,4 bonds but has α-1,6 glycosidic bonds forming branches, giving many chain ends from which enzymes can hydrolyse glucose when it is needed. Starch is large and insoluble, so it cannot leave the cell and does not lower the water potential of the cell, so water is not drawn in by osmosis. Glycogen is the storage polysaccharide of animals and fungi. Like amylopectin it contains α-1,4 and α-1,6 bonds, but it is much more highly branched with shorter chains, so it is very compact and has even more chain ends. This lets glucose be released very rapidly, which suits animals with high metabolic rates and sudden energy demands, for example in muscle and liver. It is also insoluble and osmotically inactive. Cellulose has a structural role in plant cell walls. It is made of β-glucose joined by β-1,4 glycosidic bonds; because of the position of the –OH group on carbon 1, alternate glucose units are inverted, so the chains are straight and unbranched. Straight chains lie parallel to each other and the –OH groups form very large numbers of hydrogen bonds between neighbouring chains. Chains group into microfibrils and then fibres, giving high tensile strength, so the cell wall provides support and stops the cell bursting when it is turgid. Although each hydrogen bond is weak, the strength comes from their very large number."
