    {
      "q": "Discuss how meiosis, and errors that occur during meiosis, affect the genetic make-up of offspring.",
      "marks": 16,
      "command": "Discuss",
      "modelAnswer": "Meiosis is a type of nuclear division in which one round of DNA replication is followed by two divisions, producing four haploid nuclei from one diploid cell. Halving the chromosome number is essential for sexual reproduction, because when two haploid gametes fuse at fertilisation the diploid number is restored, for example 23 + 23 = 46 in humans, rather than doubling every generation. In meiosis I homologous chromosomes are separated, and in meiosis II the centromeres divide and sister chromatids are separated. Normal meiosis generates genetic variation through recombination of alleles in two ways. In prophase I, homologous chromosomes pair up to form bivalents and non-sister chromatids break and rejoin at chiasmata, exchanging equivalent sections. This crossing over produces chromatids carrying new combinations of alleles of linked genes, although it does not create new alleles, which only arise by mutation. In metaphase I each homologous pair lines up on the equator independently of every other pair, so the combination of maternal and paternal chromosomes that ends up in each gamete is random. The number of possible combinations is 2 to the power n, where n is the haploid number, which gives about 8.4 million combinations in humans. Because crossing over makes sister chromatids different, their independent assortment in meiosis II adds further variation. Random fertilisation then combines two such gametes, so each offspring is genetically unique. This variation is the raw material for natural selection and increases the chance that some individuals in a population survive a change in the environment or a new disease, in contrast with asexual reproduction by mitosis, which produces genetically identical clones. Errors in meiosis can change the chromosome number or structure. Non-disjunction is the failure of homologous chromosomes to separate in anaphase I, or of sister chromatids to separate in anaphase II. If it occurs in meiosis I, all four gametes are abnormal, two with an extra chromosome (n + 1) and two missing one (n − 1); in meiosis II only the two gametes from the affected cell are abnormal. Fertilisation of an n + 1 gamete by a normal gamete produces a zygote with three copies of that chromosome, a form of polysomy. The best-known example is Down's syndrome, trisomy 21, with 47 chromosomes, which causes learning difficulties, characteristic facial features and a higher risk of heart defects. Its risk rises with maternal age, which may be linked to primary oocytes remaining paused in prophase I from before birth until ovulation. Fertilisation of an n − 1 gamete produces monosomy; most such zygotes do not survive, but a zygote with a single X chromosome, 45,X, develops as a female with Turner's syndrome, who is usually short and infertile because the ovaries do not develop normally. Chromosome structure can also change. In a translocation, a section of one chromosome breaks off and joins a non-homologous chromosome. A carrier of a balanced translocation has no genetic material missing or extra and is usually healthy, but at meiosis the affected chromosomes may be shared out unevenly, producing gametes with missing or extra sections, which can cause miscarriage or genetic disorders in offspring. A small proportion of Down's syndrome cases arise this way, when an extra copy of most of chromosome 21 is attached to another chromosome, often chromosome 14. In conclusion, meiosis normally maintains a constant chromosome number while generating the variation on which evolution depends, but the same process of separating chromosomes can go wrong, and because most chromosome mutations affect many genes at once, their effects on offspring are usually severe, ranging from infertility and developmental disorders to loss of the embryo."
    },
    {
      "q": "Compare sexual reproduction in mammals and in flowering plants, from the formation of gametes to the formation of the zygote.",
      "marks": 12,
      "command": "Compare",
      "modelAnswer": "In both mammals and flowering plants sexual reproduction involves meiosis, which halves the chromosome number, followed by fertilisation, which restores the diploid number and brings together genetic material from two parents. In both groups the male and female gametes are therefore haploid, and the zygote is diploid. A key difference lies in how the gametes are made. In mammals, meiosis produces the gametes directly. In spermatogenesis in the seminiferous tubules of the testes, diploid spermatogonia divide by mitosis and grow into primary spermatocytes, each of which undergoes meiosis to give four haploid spermatids that mature into motile sperm, and this continues throughout life from puberty. In oogenesis, oogonia in the fetal ovary divide by mitosis and grow into primary oocytes, which begin meiosis I before birth and pause in prophase I. From puberty, one completes meiosis I each cycle, dividing the cytoplasm unequally to give a large secondary oocyte and a small polar body, and the secondary oocyte is released at ovulation, paused at metaphase II. In flowering plants, by contrast, meiosis produces spores and the gamete nuclei are then formed by mitosis. In the anther, diploid pollen mother cells divide by meiosis to form tetrads of haploid cells, each of which develops a tough exine and a thin intine, and its nucleus divides by mitosis to form a tube nucleus and a generative nucleus. In the ovule, a megaspore mother cell divides by meiosis, three of the four haploid cells degenerate, and the nucleus of the survivor divides by mitosis three times to form an embryo sac with eight haploid nuclei, including the egg cell and two polar nuclei. The two groups are similar in that the female side produces only one functional gamete from each meiosis, with the other products degenerating, whereas the male side produces several. The way the male gamete reaches the female gamete also differs. Mammalian sperm swim through the female tract, undergo capacitation, pass the follicle cells and bind to the zona pellucida, and the acrosome releases enzymes that digest a path through it. In plants the male nuclei cannot move on their own; after pollination the pollen grain germinates on the stigma and a pollen tube grows down the style, with enzymes from its tip digesting the tissues, the tube nucleus thought to control growth, and the generative nucleus dividing by mitosis to form two male nuclei, which are released into the embryo sac through the micropyle. In both, hydrolytic enzymes are used to penetrate the tissues around the female gamete. Fertilisation itself differs. In mammals one sperm nucleus fuses with the ovum nucleus after the oocyte completes meiosis II, and the cortical reaction hardens the zona pellucida to prevent polyspermy. In flowering plants double fertilisation occurs: one male nucleus fuses with the egg to form a diploid zygote and the other fuses with the two polar nuclei to form a triploid endosperm nucleus, which develops into a food store. Mammals have no equivalent of the endosperm, because the embryo is nourished by the mother after implantation of the blastocyst. Overall, both groups use meiosis and fertilisation to combine genetic material and produce variation, but plants insert a mitotic stage between meiosis and gamete formation, deliver the male gametes through a pollen tube rather than by swimming sperm, and carry out two fusions rather than one."
    }
