      "q": "Compare allopatric and sympatric speciation, using examples to illustrate your answer.",
      "marks": 12,
      "command": "Compare",
      "modelAnswer": "Both allopatric and sympatric speciation begin when populations of one species become reproductively isolated, so that alleles no longer flow between them. In both, the isolated populations then change independently: different mutations arise in each, natural selection may favour different alleles if the selection pressures differ, and in small populations genetic drift shifts allele frequencies by chance. Both end at the same point, when the populations can no longer interbreed to produce fertile offspring. The key difference is how isolation begins. In allopatric speciation a geographical barrier such as a sea, a mountain range or a river separates the populations, or a small group colonises an island. The Galápagos finches are a classic example: birds that reached different islands were isolated and each population became adapted to the food on its island, with beak size and shape suited to particular seeds or insects. In sympatric speciation the populations share the same area and there is no physical barrier, so isolation must arise another way. In plants this is often polyploidy: an error in cell division produces offspring with extra whole sets of chromosomes that cannot produce fertile offspring with the parent species. Common cord-grass, Spartina anglica, arose when a sterile hybrid between a native and an introduced cord-grass doubled its chromosome number, becoming fertile but unable to breed with either parent. Sympatric isolation can also be ecological, as in apple maggot flies that lay eggs on apples rather than hawthorn and mate on the fruit where they developed, or behavioural and seasonal, when a change in courtship or breeding time stops one group mating with the rest. Allopatric speciation is usually slow, because divergence builds up over many generations, whereas polyploidy can produce a new species in a single generation. A further contrast is that allopatric isolation is imposed from outside by geography, while sympatric isolation arises from changes within the organisms themselves, such as chromosome number, behaviour or choice of host."
