    {
      "q": "Describe how gel electrophoresis and DNA sequencing with bioinformatics can be used to distinguish between species and to determine their evolutionary relationships.",
      "marks": 9,
      "command": "Describe",
      "modelAnswer": "In gel electrophoresis, DNA is first extracted from each sample, and a specific region may be amplified by PCR to give enough copies. The DNA is cut into fragments with restriction enzymes, which cut at specific base sequences, so DNA with different sequences gives fragments of different lengths. The fragments are loaded into wells at one end of an agarose gel covered with buffer, and a voltage is applied. Because its phosphate groups are negatively charged, DNA moves towards the positive electrode, and the gel acts as a mesh so that smaller fragments travel further in a given time. A ladder of fragments of known length is run alongside to estimate sizes, and the DNA is made visible with a stain, a fluorescent dye under UV light or labelled probes. The banding patterns of different samples are then compared: an unknown specimen whose bands match those of a known species is probably that species, and the more bands two species share, the more closely related they are likely to be. Proteins can be compared in a similar way. DNA sequencing gives more detailed evidence because it determines the exact order of bases, whereas two fragments of the same length on a gel could still differ in sequence. Biologists usually sequence the same gene in each species, choosing a slowly changing gene such as a ribosomal RNA gene to compare distant groups, or a faster-changing mitochondrial gene such as cytochrome oxidase I to separate closely related species. Bioinformatics then uses software and shared databases to store the sequences, align them and count the positions where bases differ. Because mutations build up over time, species that diverged from a common ancestor more recently show fewer differences and are more closely related. These counts are used to draw phylogenetic trees and, if the mutation rate is roughly constant, to estimate when lineages separated using a molecular clock. Comparing an unknown sequence with the database can identify a species, reveal cryptic species that look alike, or indicate a new species if no close match exists."
    },
    {
      "q": "Assess the principles of ex-situ and in-situ conservation and the issues surrounding each method.",
      "marks": 12,
      "command": "Assess",
      "modelAnswer": "In-situ conservation protects species in their natural habitat, for example in national parks, nature reserves, Sites of Special Scientific Interest and marine protected areas, while ex-situ conservation protects them away from their habitat, in zoos, botanic gardens and seed banks. Zoos contribute through captive breeding programmes that increase numbers of endangered animals. They keep studbooks and exchange animals between zoos to avoid inbreeding and retain as much genetic diversity as possible, and they can reintroduce animals to the wild, as happened with the Arabian oryx after it became extinct in the wild. Zoos also protect animals from predators, poaching and disease, and support education and research. However, captive populations are small, so genetic diversity is limited and inbreeding remains a risk; animals may behave abnormally or lose skills they need in the wild, such as finding food and avoiding predators; reintroduction fails if the original habitat has not been restored or the threat remains; and zoos are expensive and raise ethical concerns about captivity. Seed banks such as the Millennium Seed Bank store seeds of wild plants after cleaning, drying and cooling to around −20 °C, which slows metabolism so seeds stay viable for many years. They hold a great deal of genetic variety cheaply in a small space and can supply seeds for research, habitat restoration and reintroduction. Their issues are that seeds of many species, including many tropical trees, cannot survive drying and freezing, samples may not represent the whole gene pool, stored seeds do not evolve alongside changing conditions and pests, and viability must be tested regularly. In-situ protection keeps species in their natural environment, so they retain natural behaviour and interactions, whole ecosystems and many other species are protected at once, and populations continue to evolve. It may involve controlling grazing, removing invasive species, restricting development, fishing or hunting, and creating corridors between habitat fragments. Its issues are that large areas are hard to protect from poaching, illegal logging and pollution, conservation may conflict with local people who need land and resources, reserves may be small and fragmented, climate change can make a reserve unsuitable, and patrolling is expensive. On balance, in-situ conservation is preferable where it is possible, because it maintains the whole ecosystem and allows evolution to continue, but ex-situ methods are an essential safety net when wild populations are very small or their habitat is threatened. The two approaches are most effective when used together, with ex-situ populations providing individuals for reintroduction into protected habitats."
    }
