      "q": "Describe how recombinant DNA is produced and inserted into bacteria, and how bacteria containing it are identified.",
      "marks": 6,
      "command": "Describe",
      "modelAnswer": "The desired gene is first obtained, for example by cutting it from donor DNA with a restriction endonuclease or by using reverse transcriptase to make cDNA from mRNA, which has no introns and so can be expressed by bacteria. The gene and a plasmid are cut with the same restriction enzyme, which hydrolyses phosphodiester bonds at a specific recognition sequence and leaves complementary single-stranded sticky ends. When the gene and plasmid are mixed, the sticky ends base pair and DNA ligase forms phosphodiester bonds to seal the sugar–phosphate backbones, producing a recombinant plasmid. Bacteria are made to take up plasmids by transformation, for example by treating them with calcium ions and a brief heat shock, though only a small proportion of cells take one up. To identify the recombinant cells, a plasmid with two antibiotic resistance genes, such as ampicillin and tetracycline resistance, is used, with the gene inserted into the tetracycline resistance gene so that it is inactivated. The bacteria are spread on ampicillin agar, where only cells with a plasmid grow. A sterile velvet pad is pressed onto this master plate and then onto a tetracycline plate in the same orientation. Colonies that grow on ampicillin but are missing from the tetracycline plate contain the recombinant plasmid, and they are picked from the master plate and cultured."
